Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether the condition "$gf \in L^1$ for every $f \in L^p$" forces $g \in L^q$.

**Answer: Yes, $g \in L^q$ necessarily.**

## Proof

**Step 1: Define a linear operator and show it is closed.**

Define $T: L^p(\Omega) \to L^1(\Omega)$ by $Tf = gf$. This is well-defined by hypothesis and clearly linear. We show $T$ is a closed operator.

Suppose $f_n \to f$ in $L^p$ and $Tf_n \to h$ in $L^1$. Since $f_n \to f$ in $L^p$, there is a subsequence $f_{n_k} \to f$ a.e., hence $gf_{n_k} \to gf$ a.e. Since $Tf_{n_k} = gf_{n_k} \to h$ in $L^1$, a further subsequence converges to $h$ a.e. By uniqueness of a.e. limits, $h = gf$ a.e., i.e., $h = Tf$. Thus $T$ is closed.

**Step 2: Apply the Closed Graph Theorem.**

Both $L^p$ ($1 < p < \infty$) and $L^1$ are Banach spaces. By the Closed Graph Theorem, the closed linear operator $T$ is bounded: there exists $C > 0$ such that
$$\|gf\|_1 \le C\|f\|_p \quad \text{for all } f \in L^p.$$

Equivalently, the linear functional $\varphi(f) = \int_\Omega gf\,d\mu$ is bounded on $L^p$ with $\|\varphi\| \le C$.

**Step 3: Use the duality $(L^p)^* = L^q$.**

By the given duality, there exists $h \in L^q$ representing $\varphi$:
$$\int_\Omega gf\,d\mu = \int_\Omega hf\,d\mu \quad \text{for all } f \in L^p,$$
with $\|h\|_q = \|\varphi\| \le C$. Thus
$$\int_\Omega (g - h)f\,d\mu = 0 \quad \text{for all } f \in L^p.$$

**Step 4: Show $g = h$ a.e.**

First, $g$ is measurable: for any finite-measure set $E$, $\chi_E \in L^p$, so $g\chi_E \in L^1$ is measurable, giving measurability of $g$ on $E$; since $(L^p)^* = L^q$ holds (the standard setting being $\sigma$-finite), $\Omega = \bigcup_n E_n$ with $\mu(E_n) < \infty$, so $g$ is measurable on all of $\Omega$.

For any measurable $A \subseteq E_n$ (so $\mu(A) < \infty$), set
$$f = \chi_A \cdot \frac{\overline{g - h}}{|g - h|} \quad (\text{with } 0/0 := 0).$$
Then $|f| \le \chi_A$, so $f \in L^p$, and $(g-h)f = |g-h|\cdot\chi_A$. The identity $\int_\Omega (g-h)f\,d\mu = 0$ gives
$$\int_A |g - h|\,d\mu = 0,$$
so $g = h$ a.e. on $A$, hence on $E_n$. Taking the union over $n$, $g = h$ a.e. on $\Omega$.

Since $h \in L^q$ and $g = h$ a.e., we conclude $g \in L^q$ with $\|g\|_q = \|h\|_q \le C$. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
